    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Talents Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Perform in the Fiestas Patrias Activities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay complex information from teachers to students.<br>concept: Explain basic concepts using real-life examples.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain basic concepts about Fiestas Patrias, using real-life examples and visuals (e.g., show a picture of students rehearsing a folkloric dance and write, 'Practice the dance steps.'). Can relay teacher instructions for Fiestas Patrias events by writing simple steps and using visuals (e.g., point to a clock drawing or a picture of the school gate and write, 'Meet at the school gate at 8 a.m.'). Can simplify parade preparation tasks using written steps and pictures (e.g., show an image of a student carrying the Panamanian flag in the parade and write, 'Hold the flag with both hands.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a set of picture flashcards related to Fiestas Patrias (e.g., a flag, a band marching, students dancing, a baton twirler). Students quickly call out the vocabulary words. The teacher then shows a complex sentence about Fiestas Patrias preparations (e.g., "The director requires all participants to arrive promptly at 7:30 AM at the school entrance for the parade."). Students are asked: "How can we make this sentence easier to understand for everyone?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher demonstrates how to simplify the complex sentence using visuals and fewer words. The teacher writes the complex sentence on the board and then writes below it: "Meet at school gate, 7:30 AM" while showing a picture of a school gate and a clock. The teacher explains: "I made it simpler, right? I used fewer words and a picture. This is called 'mediation' – making information easy to understand."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I mediate, do I make information more difficult or easier to understand?" (Expected answer: Easier!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I use pictures and simple words when I mediate?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Talents Around Us"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A written "Teacher's Memo for Fiestas Patrias Parade" containing detailed instructions (genre: formal instructions, register: semi-formal, format: list of requirements). Example sentences include: "All band members must ensure their uniforms are clean and pressed for the event," and "Students selected for the honor roll will receive their flags at 7:00 AM sharp." The memo also includes specific roles for the Fiestas Patrias Parade Planner project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Relay complex information from teachers to students; explain basic concepts using real-life examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher distributes the memo and reads it aloud. Students listen and follow along. Then, they answer gist questions orally:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this memo about?" (Expected answer: The Fiestas Patrias parade.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who needs to do things for the parade?" (Expected answer: Band members, honor roll students, baton twirlers, performers, students.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing we need to remember for the parade?" (Expected answer: Clean uniforms, be on time, practice, etc.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying complex instructions from the "Teacher's Memo for Fiestas Patrias Parade" using target grammar (future simple, first conditional, modal 'can') and vocabulary. They will relay information clearly and concisely, incorporating visuals.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Future simple tense for plans (e.g., 'I will play the flute.'), First conditional (e.g., 'If I practice, I will perform.'), Modal 'can' (e.g., 'I can play the drums.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> parade, flag, honor roll student, band, baton twirler, talent, show, stage, performance, practice, event, skill, prepare, show, play, act, sing, dance, perform, march, wave, twirl, need to, have to, try, rehearse, excited, talented, ready, nervous, amazing, best, engaging, entertaining, artistic, fun, traditional, patriotic, colorful, my, your, our, their.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simplify & Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with complex sentences from the "Teacher's Memo" on one side and a column for simplified sentences with small picture icons on the other. Students match the complex sentence to a simple one and draw a quick icon.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "All participants must ensure their costumes are clean and in good condition." → "Clean your costume." (Picture of a clean shirt).
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "It is imperative that all performers rehearse their routines consistently." → "Practice the dance steps." (Picture of a person dancing).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Builder with Visuals (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays various picture prompts related to the parade roles/tasks (e.g., a student carrying a flag, a drum, a flute, students marching, a baton twirler). Students work in pairs to write a simple instruction or a plan for each visual using the target grammar.
                        <br><i>Example Prompt (picture of student with flag):</i> "I will carry the flag."
                        <br><i>Example Prompt (picture of drums):</i> "I can play the drums."
                        <br><i>Example Prompt (picture of practicing):</i> "If you practice, you will perform beautifully."
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Explain Your Role" Relay (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students are given a specific "parade role" or "task" from the original memo (e.g., "Flag Bearer," "Band Member," "Baton Twirler," "Audience Organizer"). In pairs, Student A reads a slightly complex instruction for their role (e.g., "The Flag Bearer should hold the flag securely with both hands during the entire march."). Student B must simplify it using minimal words and suggest a visual (e.g., "Hold the flag with both hands." + pantomime holding a flag). Then they switch roles.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Talents Around Us"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: Fiestas Patrias Parade Planner - Simplified Instructions.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is assigned a section of the mock Fiestas Patrias Parade Planner project (e.g., "Parade Roles," "Event Schedule," "Preparation Rules," "Performance Guidelines"). Using the original "Teacher's Memo" and their practice from Stage 3, their task is to mediate their section, creating a simple, easy-to-understand "Instruction Card" or "Poster" using written steps, simple sentences, and relevant visuals (drawings, cut-outs, or digital images). They should aim for clarity for a 6th-grade audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each group will present their simplified "Instruction Card" or "Poster" to the class, orally explaining their section of the parade planner. They will use the target grammar (future simple, first conditional, 'can') and vocabulary. For example, a group presenting "Parade Roles" might say: "This is our plan for the parade. [Student 1] will carry the flag. [Student 2] can play the drums. If we work together, our parade will be amazing!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher circulates, offering support, checking for use of simple language and visuals, and prompting students to use the target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher ensures respectful collaboration with polite language to assign tasks (e.g., 'Can you help draw the flag?' 'Let's write this step simply.').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Fiestas Patrias Parade Planner - Simplified Instruction Card/Poster" to the class. Each group explains their section, ensuring the information is clear and easy for their peers to understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of relayed information (easy to understand for classmates).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals (pictures/drawings) and simple written steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent use of target grammar (future simple, first conditional, modal 'can').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Fiestas Patrias vocabulary (e.g., parade, flag, perform, practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation and respectful language within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was my group's explanation clear for my classmates? Why or why not?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new way I learned to make information easier to understand today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did working with my team help me make the parade plan easy to understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining instructions did I do best today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students successfully engaged in simplifying instructions and concepts, especially when using visuals. The "Explain Your Role" relay helped reinforce the practical application of mediation. Some students still need more scaffolding with the first conditional structures; next time, more sentence frames and a clear example of cause-and-effect with parade outcomes ("If we arrive late, we will miss the start.") will be beneficial. The project-based learning for the Fiestas Patrias Parade Planner was highly motivating and provided a real-life context for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "The Talents Around Us" and preparing for Fiestas Patrias. The skills of simplifying and relaying information are crucial for communicating in all aspects of life, and we will continue to use them in our next theme, where we explore local community events.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>